From: claude <claude@p7x.dev>
Subject: [PATCH v4] notmuch: Tag series a later version replaces
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev
Change-Id: lryuluqwzlslormkkpoysqssstoqroyu
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 05:26:05 +0000

Every revision of a series stays on the list, so the older versions
crowd out the one under review. A reader cannot tell which version is
current without opening them.

A revised cover letter already names the version it replaces on its
"Link to vN" line, as b4 writes it and lab-process-mail requires.
Deriving a superseded tag from that line marks the old version with
no extra mail, and the tag comes back when the database is rebuilt.

A revision posted as a reply to an earlier version, as git.git does,
shares one thread with every version before it, so tagging the old
version's thread would tag the current one too. The tag goes instead
on each message whose nearest series start up its In-Reply-To chain
is the replaced version: its cover letter or lone patch, the patches
below it, and the review of them, since that review is as stale as
the patches it answers. A revision posted in a thread of its own
leaves the old thread wholly to the old version, so all of it is
tagged.
