change-id: lryuluqwzlslormkkpoysqssstoqroyu

Pat chose git.git's threading for revisions in "Re: [RFC] threading
strategies for RFC and patch versions" [1], so a revision replies to
the first version and every version shares one thread. v3 tags the
whole thread a Link line names, which would then tag the current
version too. This version tags each replaced version and its review,
and leaves the rest of the thread alone. Apply it before, or with,
"[PATCH 0/3] Thread each revision under its first version" [2],
which starts posting revisions that way; it applies to b/dev@upstream
on its own.

I tested it on a scratch maildir holding v1, v2, and v3 of one series
in a single thread, each with patches and a review, plus a legacy v2
in its own thread. It tags v1, v2, and their reviews, and leaves v3
and its review, and the legacy v2, untagged. On a copy of the list it
tags 183 messages in 41 threads, the same as v3 less one: a reply in
"[PATCH] doc: Add a guide to the patch workflow" with no Message-ID
header, which notmuch tag cannot name. A second run tags the same
set.

